		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=3804&amp;p=37168#p37168"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Hello everyone. Unstable operation of the access point on the RD-5R is not related to USB interference. It is related to a power supply sag during transmission. I tried connecting an external power supply. At 9 volts, 1.5 amps and a power of 250 mW, everything has been working stably for three hours. I do not claim that I have found a solution, but for now it works. From the battery, the port fails depending on the battery charge.</div></blockquote>9V is much higher than the normal maximum voltage from the batteries, i.e max voltage when fully charged is usually 8.2V<br>Using 9V will cause the internal 5V and 3.3V voltage regulators to generate more heat than they are designed to dissipate.<br><br>Did you try using 8V or less, which is the normal operating voltage from batteries.?<br><br>Does it work at higher power levels using the external PSU?<br><br>Did you measure the Tx power ?  i.e The calibration data in the RD5R is not normally accurate and your output power may be higher or lower than this value. i.e possibly much lower<br><br>Also. Is the 250mW setting the maximum stable power even when suppied from an external PSU?<br><br><br><br>Overall, it still appears that Hotspot mode is not stable on the RD5R in its normal operating configuation.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Sun May 18, 2025 10:59 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
